language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_049_number_partitioning: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Find a partition of the numbers 1 to N into two sets A and B such that A and B have the same cardinality, the sum of the numbers in A equals the sum of the numbers in B, and the sum of the squares of the numbers in A equals the sum of the squares of the numbers in B. There is no solution for N < 8.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_049_number_partitioning.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_049_number_partitioning.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_049_number_partitioning_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_049_number_partitioning.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_049_number_partitioning_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the numbers are 1 to n (N in the problem), n even
given n : int(1..)
$ half: the size of each set, n / 2
letting half = n / 2

$ A[i]: the i-th number of set A, the sets listed in increasing order (indices 1-based)
find A : matrix indexed by [int(1..half)] of int(1..n)
$ B[i]: the i-th number of set B, the sets listed in increasing order (indices 1-based)
find B : matrix indexed by [int(1..half)] of int(1..n)

branching on [A, B]
such that
$ the numbers within each set are distinct
allDiff(A),
allDiff(B),
$ no number is in both sets, so A and B together hold each of the numbers 1 to n exactly once
forAll i : int(1..half) . forAll j : int(1..half) . (A[i] != B[j]),
$ the two sets have the same cardinality (both have half elements, by the declaration)
$ the sum of the numbers in A equals the sum of the numbers in B
(sum i : int(1..half) . A[i]) = (sum i : int(1..half) . B[i]),
$ the sum of the squares of the numbers in A equals the sum of the squares of the numbers in B
(sum i : int(1..half) . A[i] * A[i]) = (sum i : int(1..half) . B[i] * B[i]),
$ symmetry breaking: a set has no order, so its numbers are listed in increasing order
forAll i : int(1..half-1) . (A[i] < A[i+1]),
forAll i : int(1..half-1) . (B[i] < B[i+1])
